# Caddy in front of the Stock Digest UI on a server: HTTPS plus a password per person.
#
# The app keeps listening on 127.0.0.1:8765 only (run `stock-digest --no-browser`), so
# Caddy is the only way in. Every search spends the server's OpenAI and social-provider
# credits; keep basic_auth on.
#
# 1. Replace digest.example.com with your domain. Its DNS A record must point at the
#    server, and ports 80 and 443 must be open; Caddy obtains and renews the certificate.
# 2. For each person, run `caddy hash-password` and add a line: <username> <hash>.
#    The placeholder below is not a valid hash, so it lets nobody in until you replace it.
# 3. Install as /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, check it with
#    `caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile`, then `systemctl reload caddy`.
#
# Progress streams as newline-delimited JSON without a Content-Length, which Caddy flushes
# immediately. Do not add `flush_interval -1`: in that mode Caddy keeps the backend request
# running after the browser disconnects, so an abandoned search would keep spending.

digest.example.com {
	basic_auth {
		replace-me REPLACE_WITH_OUTPUT_OF_caddy_hash-password
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8765
}
